  "name": null,
  "problem": "When Amy's knight reaches level $k,$ he has $k+2$ swords. However, a knight can only use $3$ swords each day. \n\nFind a **polynomial** expression, in terms of $k$, for the number of ways Amy can use her swords on a particular day when at level $k.$ You may assume that the order in which the swords are used does not matter.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The number of combinations equals \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "Since the order in which the swords are used does not matter, the number of ways the swords can be used is a combination of the available swords.\n\nThe number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nWhen at level $k,$ there are $k+2$ swords, so $n=k+2.$ We want to select $3$ of those, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different selections of three swords is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{k+2}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{(k+2)!}{((k+2)-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot (\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 5, we found that $\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}={k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{k+2}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot (\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}({k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k) \\\\  & =\\frac{{k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
